.SH "Introduction"
.PP
The glados is a third year Epitech project where five people are asked to build a new programming language.

.PP
Who never had problems with a programming language ?
Who never thought of building it’s own ?
Who never imagined a language so litteral, that anyone could use it without technical knowledge ?

.PP
Introducing: the \fBFranc C\fP.
The Franc C is a programming language with a litteral french syntax.

.PP
In this project, we built five different things.
.IP \(em 4
The Franc C language itself, defining it’s grammar and application
.IP \(em 4
A bytecode associated with the Franc C, used to run Franc C Programs
.IP \(em 4
A virtual machine reference, used to dictate how the bytecode should be executed.
.IP \(em 4
The Franc C Compiler, a tool used to compile Franc C Reference code into a Franc C Program
.IP \(em 4
The Franc C Virtual Machine, a tool used to run Franc C Program on any computer.
.IP \(em 4
The Larousse, a library of Franc C Reference code used to help Franc C programmers with some basic utilitarian functions
.SH "Franc C reference"
.SS "Motives"
.IP \(em 4
The \fBFranc C\fP was built to allow french people to program in their
home language.
.IP \(em 4
The language is easily readable for a non-technical person.
.IP \(em 4
The focus is put on the syntax, not in the functionalities of the
language.
.SS "Inspirations"
.PP
We took inspiration for our syntax on a beautiful language,
http://langagelinotte.free.fr \fBat\fP \fIlinotte\fP ; and for our implementation
on the https://www.iso.org/standard/82075.html \fBat\fP \fIC programming
language\fP.

.PP
Obviously, the french language is our biggest inspirator.
.SS "Linotte"
.PP
The linotte programming language is a high-level,
french-litteral-syntaxed programming language made for introducing
programmation to children.

.PP
We saw it as a great inspiration for our language with the next
features:

.IP \(em 4
Word delimitation at the end of functions, if and while conditionals.
.IP \(em 4
Use of some syntax like “read” instead of “execute” code

.PP
The linotte syntax was, however, not litteral enough to our liking.
.SS "C"
.PP
The C programming language is a low-level language. We took inspiration
from it in the implementation of our language. For example, the C
programming language is from the imperative paradigm, and so is our
language.

.PP
We took many things from this language:

.IP \(em 4
The imperative paradigm
.IP \(em 4
The int-defined booleans In the C programming language, all integers
can be interpreted as booleans. A false is a value strictly equal to
zero, and a true is every other values.
.IP \(em 4
The possibility of defining variables local to each function call
.IP \(em 4
The possibility of using recursive function calls
.IP \(em 4
The representation of a chararacter by it’s ascii value
.IP \(em 4
The function used to print a character “putchar”
.IP \(em 4
The existence of a standard library with some useful functions
.IP \(em 4
All of the operations like xor, addition, bitshift...
.IP \(em 4
The possibility for functions to return a value
.IP \(em 4
The infix notation of operations, with parenthesis
.IP \(em 4
The possibility to compute directly with any combination of operations
in any part of the code where a value is required. For example, when
passing a parameter, you could pass a computation, a variable value,
or a mix of both

.PP
Many of these features are also present in other languages, but we took
them from our knowledge of the C programming language.
.SS "French language"
.PP
French is not a programming, but a spoken language.

.PP
We wanted a truly grammatically correct, complete, easily readable, and
beautiful language.

.PP
Our choice was obviously french, as french syntax is way more precise
than english’s.

.PP
We searched for dozen of grammar rules to inforce in our language.
.SS "Language Formal Syntax (BNF)"
.RS
.nf
\fC<header> ::= "Bonjour," <spaces>

<main> ::= "En" <spaces> "sachant" <spaces> "que" <spaces> "les" <spaces> "variables" <spaces> "principales" <spaces> "sont" <spaces> ":" <spaces> [<variable_list>]
 "\e;" <spaces> "pourrais-tu" <spaces> "s'il" <spaces> "te" <spaces> "plaît" <spaces> "commencer" <spaces> "la" <spaces> "lecture" <spaces> "ici" <spaces> "?" [<spaces>] <block> [<spaces>] "Merci d'avance," <spaces> "Cordialement," <spaces> <name> <name>

<variable_definition> ::= "-" <spaces> <word> "," <spaces> "de" <spaces> "type" <spaces> <variable_type> "," <spaces> "valant" <spaces> <value> 

<variable_list> ::= <variable_definition> <spaces> [<variable_list>]

<exclusif_or> ::= "ou" <spaces> "exclusif" | "xor"

<arithmetic_operator> ::= "plus" | "moins"
                        | ("divisé" | "multiplié") <spaces> "par"
                        | "modulo"
                        | "fois"

<comparison_operator> ::= "est"
                        | "est" <spaces> "égale" <spaces> "à"
                        | "est" <spaces> "différent" <spaces> "de"
                        | "est" <spaces> "inférieure" <spaces> "à"
                        | "est" <spaces> "supérieure" <spaces> "à"
                        | "est" <spaces> "inférieure" <spaces> "ou" <spaces> "égale" <spaces> "à"
                        | "est" <spaces> "supérieure" <spaces> "ou" <spaces> "égale" <spaces> "à"

<logical_operator> ::= "et" | "ou"

<bitwise_operator> ::= "décalé" <spaces> "binairement" <spaces> "à" <spaces> "gauche"
    | "décalé" <spaces> "binairement" <spaces> "à" <spaces> "droite"
    | "et" <spaces> "binaire"
    | "ou" <spaces> "binaire"
    | <exclusif_or>
 
<operators> ::= <bitwise_operator> | <logical_operator> | <comparison_operator> | <arithmetic_operator>

<parenthesized_condition> ::= "(" <condition> ")"

<condition> ::= <word> [<spaces> <operators> <spaces> (<condition> | <parenthesized_condition>)]
              | <value> [<spaces> <operators> <spaces> (<condition> | <parenthesized_condition>)]

<parameter> ::= "-" <spaces> <word> "," <spaces> "de" <spaces> "type" <spaces> <variable_type>

<parameter_list> ::= <parameter> <spaces> [<parameter_list>]

<function_definition> ::= "J’aimerais" <spaces> "définir" <spaces> "le" <spaces> "bloc" <spaces> "répondant" <spaces> "au" <spaces> "nom" <spaces> "de" <spaces> <word> "," <spaces> "de" <spaces> "type" <spaces> "de" <spaces> "retour" <spaces> <function_type> "," <spaces> "nécessitant" <spaces> "comme" <spaces> "entrée" <spaces> ":" <spaces> [<parameter_list>] "\e;" <spaces> "contenant" <spaces> "les" <spaces> "variables" <spaces> ":" <spaces> [<variable_list>] "\e;" <spaces> "représenté" <spaces> "par" <spaces> "le" <spaces> "code" <spaces> ("suivant" | "ci-après" | "ci-dessous") "." [<spaces>] <block> <spaces> "Merci."

<text> ::= <symbol> [<text>]

<comment> ::= "*" <text> "*"

<quoted_value> ::= "« " <text> " »"

<block> ::= <statement_list>

<statement_list> ::= <statement> [[<spaces>] <statement_list> [<spaces]]

<statement> ::= <condition_statement> | <return_statement> | <loop_statement> | <display_statement> | <invoke_statement> | <assignment_statement>

<return_statement> ::= "Enfin," <spaces> "renvoie" <spaces> <condition> "." | "Enfin," <spaces> "sors" <spaces> "du" <spaces> "bloc."

<condition_definition> ::= "Si" <spaces> <condition> "," <spaces> "exécute" <spaces> "le" <spaces> "texte" <spaces> ":"

<condition_statement> ::= <condition_definition> <space> <block> "Merci."
    | <condition_definition> <space> <block> "\e;" <space> "sinon" <space> "exécute" <space> "le" <space> "texte" <space> ":" <block> "Merci."

<loop_statement> ::= "Tant" <spaces> "que" <condition> <spaces> "exécute" <spaces> "le" <spaces> "code" <spaces> "ci-après" <spaces> ":" <spaces> <block> "Merci."

<display_statement> ::= "Affiche" (<condition> | <quoted_value>) "."

<invoke_params> ::= <condition> ["," <condition>]

<invoke_statement> ::= "J'invoque" <spaces> "le" <spaces> "bloc" <spaces> <word>
    ["," <spaces> "et" <spaces> "j'assigne" <spaces> "la" <spaces> "valeur" <spaces> "de" <spaces> "retour" <spaces> "à" <spaces> "la" <spaces> "variable" <spaces> <word>]
    ["," <spaces> "avec les paramètres" <spaces> <invoke_params>] "."

<assignment_statement> ::= "J'aimerais" <spaces> "que" <spaces> <word> <spaces> "prenne" <spaces> "la" <spaces> "valeur" <spaces> <condition> "." |
    <word> <spaces> "prend" <spaces> "la" <spaces> "valeur" <spaces> <condition>

<variable_type> ::= "entier" <spaces> "naturel" | "booléen"

<function_type> ::= "entier" <spaces> "naturel" | "booléen" | "nul"

<name> ::= <word> "\en"

<variables_list> ::= <variables> | <variables> <spaces> <variables_list>

<variables> ::= <name> "," <spaces> "de" <spaces> "type" <spaces> <type> "," <spaces> "valant" <spaces> <value>

<space> ::= "\et" | "\en" | "\er" | "\ef" | "\ev"

<spaces> ::= <space> | <space> <spaces>

<number> ::= 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | ...

<signed_number> ::= "-" <number> | "~" <number> | "non" <spaces> <number>

<value> ::= <signed_number> | <number> | <bool> | <char_value>

<word_char> ::= <printable> | <word_symbol>

<printable> ::= "A" .. "Z"
              | "a" .. "z"
              | "0" .. "9"

<word_symbol> ::= "!" | "\e"" | "#" | "$" | "%" | "&" | "'" | "*" | "+" | "-" | "/" 
           | ":" | "\e;" | "<" | "=" | ">" | "?" | "@"
           | "[" | "\e\e" | "]" | "^" | "_" | "{" | "}" | "|" | "~"

<symbol> ::= <word_symbol> | <space> | <printable> | "," | "\et" | "\en" | "(" | ")"

<word> ::= <word_char> | <word_char> <word>

<char_value> ::= "'" <symbol> "'"

<bool> ::= "faux" | "fausse" | "vrai" | "vraie"
\fP
.fi
.RE

.PP
As the language is heavily based on the \fBFrench language\fP, it has shaped
many of our syntax decisions :

.IP \(em 4
We made it mandatory to put spaces where needed in case of
punctiation, as it is a
https://formations.mer.gouv.fr/sites/default/files/2023-11/Espacement%20avant%20et%20apr%C3%A8s%20les%20signes%20de%20ponctuation%20et%20les%20symboles.pdf \fBat\fP \fIfrench
grammar rule\fP. This works for (’:’, ’.’, ’«’, ’»’, ’,’).
.IP \(em 4
We decided to replace all operations symbols by their literal French
equivalent as we wanted this language to use \fBas many french words as
possible\fP in order to represent \fBFrance\fP. Therefore, operations such
as \fI+\fP or \fI*\fP are being replaced by \fIplus\fP and \fImultiplié par\fP. This
forces to have at least one space before and after such operations as
they are now depicted as \fBwords\fP.
.IP \(em 4
As it can become easily overwhelming to type out every single
operation using its own word representation, we created alternatives
to some operations to make them shorter. For example, the XOR \fI^\fP
operation can either be typed out as \fIexclusif ou\fP or \fIxor\fP. The
multiply \fI*\fP operation can also either be \fImultiplié par\fP or \fIfois\fP.
.IP \(em 4
We forced every file of our language to begin with \fIBonjour,\fP (\fIHello\fP
in english) in order to fit with the French Culture as french citizens
are widely known for their politeness. For the same reasons, \fIwhile\fP
and \fIif\fP structures ends by \fIMerci.\fP (\fIThank you=in english) and the
  main function ends with
  =Merci d'avance, Cordialement, <Name>\n<FamilyName>\fP (for
\fIThank you in advance, Best regards, <Name>\n<FamilyName>\fP) as this
function is where the program stops.
.IP \(em 4
Words used to describe instructions such as \fIJ'invoque\fP (\fIInvoke\fP in
english) or \fIAffiche\fP (\fIDisplay\fP in english) were what we thought was
the best way to directly translate such common instructions in
\fBfrench\fP.
.IP \(em 4
As we wanted users to have a unique experience while using the
language, instructions directly made by users are conjugated in the
first person in order to simulate a conversation. Other instructions
executed directly by the machine such as displaying are in imperative
form in the second person.
.IP \(em 4
Every single instructions ends with a \fI.\fP as every single sentence in
\fBfrench\fP ends the same way.
.IP \(em 4
\fIwhile\fP and \fIif\fP structures do not exactly share the same syntax in
order to differentiate them properly : \fIif\fP takes a \fI,\fP at the end of
the condition whereas \fIwhile\fP doesn’t.
.IP \(em 4
Comparison are in the feminine form (\fIsupérieure\fP, \fIégale\fP) since the
French word \fIvariable\fP is feminine.
.SS "Language logic"
.RS
.TP
.ft I
\fBHeader\fP
.ft
.PP
A file in \fIFranc C\fP always starts with: “Bonjour,” followed by an empty
line.

.PP
Politeness is very important, and we wanted \fIFranc C\fP to express a
proper and classic language, not a crappy street language. We promise
you will learn the “bien parlé” by using \fIFranc C\fP
.RE
.TP
.ft I
\fBFunction Declaration\fP
.ft
.PP
In order to declare a function, you must specify:
.RS
.TP
.ft I
The name of the function
.ft
.RS
.nf
J'aimerais définir le bloc répondant au nom de <function_name>,

.fi
.RE

.PP
where is the name of the function
.RE
.TP
.ft I
The return type of the function
.ft
.RS
.nf
de type de retour entier naturel,

.fi
.RE

.PP
for the function to return an integer.

.RS
.nf
de type de retour booléen,

.fi
.RE

.PP
for the function to return a boolean.

.RS
.nf
de type de retour nul,

.fi
.RE

.PP
if the function does not return anything. (void function)
.RE
.TP
.ft I
The parameters of the function
.ft
.PP
A parameter, in Franc C, is defined the same way as other programming
languages : Those are variables that must be passed when the function is
invoked for it to use them.

.RS
.nf
necéssitant comme entrée :
    - <variable_name1>, de type <type>
    - <variable_name2>, de type <type>
    ...
;

.fi
.RE

.PP
You can include as many parameter as you want as long as they \fBdo not
share\fP the same name.

.IP \(em 4
\fI<variable_name1>\fP and \fI<variable_name2>\fP are the name of the
variables which are both different.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<type>\fP is either \fIbooléen\fP if the parameter is a \fIboolean\fP or
\fIentier naturel\fP if it is an \fIint\fP.

.PP
Parameters are obviously optional. If the function does not take any
parameters, you must specify :

.RS
.nf
nécessitant comme entrée : ;

.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Variables
.ft
.PP
Variables created and used inside a function, in Franc C, are defined in
the function definition. Since Franc C is a very verbose language and
uses a lot of words, creating variables at the top of the function makes
it a very good way to clearly see them.

.PP
Such variables are being declared the following way :

.RS
.nf
contenant les variables :
    - <variable_name1>, de type <type>, valant <value>
    - <variable_name2>, de type <type>, valant <value>
;

.fi
.RE

.PP
Just like \fBparameters\fP, \fBvariables\fP must not share the same name. A
\fBparameter\fP and a \fBvariable\fP can not share the same name either.

.IP \(em 4
\fI<variable_name1>\fP and \fI<variable_name2>\fP are the name of the
variables which are both different.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<type>\fP is either \fIbooléen\fP if the variable is a \fIboolean\fP or
\fIentier naturel\fP if it is an \fIint\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<value>\fP is either a number or a boolean expression \fIvrai\fP \fIvraie\fP
\fIfaux\fP or \fIfausse\fP. It can also be a character value like \fI\n\fP (which
will be 10).

.PP
This \fBforces\fP every single created variable to have a default value.

.PP
If the function does not use any variable, you must specify :

.RS
.nf
contenant les variables : ;

.fi
.RE
.RE
.RE
.TP
.ft I
\fBFunction Body\fP
.ft
.PP
A function declaration ends with

.RS
.nf
représenté par le code <logical_connector>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<logical_connector>\fP is either \fIci\-après\fP, \fIsuivant\fP, or
\fIci\-dessous\fP.

.PP
What must follow this declaration is the \fBbody of the function\fP, which
is what \fBwill be executed\fP when the function is \fBinvoked\fP.

.PP
In Franc C, what can be executed inside a function is being refered as
an \fIinstruction\fP.

.PP
Those are the available instructions that you can execute inside a
function :
.RS
.TP
.ft I
Assignement
.ft
.PP
You can, at any time, change the value of a variable the following way :

.RS
.nf
J'aimerais que <variable_name> prenne la valeur <computables>.

.fi
.RE

.PP
or

.RS
.nf
<variable_name> prends la valeur <computables>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<variable_name>\fP represents the name of an existing well-defined
variable of the function.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)
.RE
.TP
.ft I
If
.ft
.PP
Execute a block of code only if a specific condition was met. It can be
defined the following way :

.RS
.nf
Si <computables>, exécute le texte :
    <more_instructions>
Merci.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)
.IP \(em 4
\fI<more_instructions>\fP represents all the instructions that will be
executed \fBONLY\fP if the \fBcondition\fP was \fBmet\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fIMerci.\fP represents the end of the condition.

.PP
You can also execute a block of code if the condition wasn’t met the
following way :

.RS
.nf
Si <computables>, exécute le texte :
    <more_instructions>
; sinon, exécute le texte :
    <more_instructions_2>
Merci.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)
.IP \(em 4
\fI<more_instructions>\fP represents all the instructions that will be
executed \fBONLY\fP if the \fBcondition\fP was \fBmet\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<more_instructions_2>\fP represents all the instructions that will be
executed \fBONLY\fP if the \fBcondition\fP was \fBnot met\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fIMerci.\fP represents the end of the condition.
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Invoke
.ft
.PP
Invoke another function inside a function, you can also store its result
in a well-defined function variable.

.RS
.nf
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<function_name>\fP is the name of an \fBexisting defined function\fP.

.PP
If the function that is being invoked takes parameters, they \fBmust\fP be
specified the following way :

.RS
.nf
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>, avec les paramètres <computables>, <computables>, <computables>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<function_name>\fP is the name of an \fBexisting defined function\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)

.PP
You \fBmust\fP not give to a function more parameters than it takes.

.PP
In order to store the result of a function, while still giving
parameters :

.RS
.nf
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>, et j'assigne la valeur de retour à la variable <variable_name>, avec les paramètres <computables>, <computables>, <computables>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<function_name>\fP is the name of an \fBexisting defined function\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<variable_name>\fP represents the name of an existing well-defined
variable of the function.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)

.PP
However, if you do not want to give any parameter :

.RS
.nf
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>, et j'assigne la valeur de retour à la variable <variable_name>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<function_name>\fP is the name of an \fBexisting defined function\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<variable_name>\fP represents the name of an existing well-defined
variable of the function
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Loop
.ft
.PP
The only type of loop that is currently implemented in \fIFranc C\fP are the
\fIwhile\fP loops.

.PP
A loop allows to execute a block of code until a condition cannot be met
anymore.

.RS
.nf
Tant que <computables> exécute le code ci-après :
    <more_instructions>
Merci.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)
.IP \(em 4
\fI<more_instructions>\fP represents all the instructions that will be
executed \fBONLY\fP if the \fBcondition\fP was \fBmet\fP.
.IP \(em 4
\fIMerci.\fP represents the end of the loop.
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Return
.ft
.PP
During the execution of the function, you can return at any time a
\fBvalue\fP. Returning a value will cause the function execution to \fBstop\fP.

.PP
The only condition to return a value is that the function must not be of
type “nul”.

.RS
.nf
Enfin, renvoie <computables>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)

.PP
If the function does not return anything (\fInul\fP function), you can still
stop the execution at any time.

.RS
.nf
Enfin, sors du bloc.

.fi
.RE

.PP
This works only if the function is a \fInul\fP function.
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Show statements
.ft
.PP
This instruction is used to display either a text, or the result of a
computation in the screen.

.PP
To display a computation :

.RS
.nf
Affiche <computables>.

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<computables>\fP represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the \fI<condition>\fP defintion on the BNF)

.PP
This will display \fBthe character\fP corresponding to the result on the
screen. (E.g): \fIAffiche 10.\fP will display \fI\n\fP.

.PP
However, if you want to display a text :

.RS
.nf
Affiche « Meow ».

.fi
.RE

.PP
This will display the text \fIMeow\fP on the screen.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.ft I
\fBEnd of Function\fP
.ft
.PP
You must \fBalways\fP end your \fBFunction Body\fP with a \fIMerci.\fP.
.RE
.TP
.ft I
\fBSpecial case of ’Main’ Function\fP
.ft
.PP
The \fBmain function\fP is where the program starts. Therefore, its
defintion must be clearly different from the \fBother functions\fP.

.PP
Such functions are being defined the following way :

.RS
.nf
Bonjour,

En sachant que les variables principales sont :
    <variables>
; pourrais-tu s'il te plaît commencer la lecture ici ?
    <instructions>
Merci d'avance,
Cordialement,
<family_name>
<name>

.fi
.RE

.IP \(em 4
\fI<variables>\fP is an optional field, and represents the variables of
the function. They are declared the same way as a normal function.
.IP \(em 4
\fI<instructions>\fP represents all the instructions that will be
executed.
.IP \(em 4
\fIMerci d'avance, Cordialement, <family_name> <name>.\fP represents the
end of the main function
.IP \(em 4
\fI<family_name>\fP represents the developer’s last name, and must always
end with a newline character (’’).
.IP \(em 4
\fI<name>\fP represents the developer’s first name, and must also end with
a newline character (’’).

.PP
A \fBmain function\fP does not take any parameter. Therefore, no parameter
definition must be done. The value being \fBreturned\fP in the \fBmain
function\fP represent the exit status of the program. If no value was
being \fBreturned\fP, the program exists by default with a status code of
\fI0\fP.
.RE
.SH "Bytecode reference"
.PP
The bytecode starts with a magic number, followed by an address
describing the placement of the PC at the start of the program, followed
by the program itself.
.SS "Formal syntax (BNF)"
.RS
.nf
\fC<bytecode> ::= <magic_number> <adress> <operation_list>

<magic_number> ::= "E\efE\ef" ; 0x45 0xc 0x45 0xc

<adress> ::= <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte>

<operation_list> ::= <operation> | <operation>  <operation_list>

<operation> ::= <not>
              | <lnot>
              | <neg>
              | <and>
              | <or>
              | <xor>
              | <shl>
              | <shr>
              | <land>
              | <lor>
              | <add>
              | <sub>
              | <mult>
              | <div>
              | <mod>
              | <gt>
              | <ge>
              | <lt>
              | <le>
              | <eq>
              | <diff>
              | <updz>
              | <push_stack>
              | <pop_stack>
              | <pop>
              | <call>
              | <ret>
              | <jmp>
              | <zjmp>
              | <aff>
              | <affs>
              | <push_value>
              | <push_label>


<not> ::= "\e003"

<lnot> ::= "\e004"

<neg> ::= "\e005"

<and> ::= "\e006"

<or> ::= "\ea"

<land> ::= "\eb"

<lor> ::= "\et"

<xor> ::= "\en"

<shl> ::= "\ev"

<shr> ::= "\ef"

<add> ::= "\er"

<sub> ::= "\e016"

<mult> ::= "\e017"

<div> ::= "\e020"

<mod> ::= "\e021"

<gt> ::= "\e022"

<ge> ::= "\e023"

<lt> ::= "\e024"

<le> ::= "\e025"

<eq> ::= "\e026"

<diff> ::= "\e027"

<updz> ::= "\e030"

<push_stack> ::= "\e035" <arguments>

<pop_stack> ::= "\e036" <arguments>

<pop> ::= "\e037"

<call> ::= """

<ret> ::= "#"

<jmp> ::= "$"

<zjmp> ::= "%"

<aff> ::= "&"

<affs> ::= "'" <arguments> <string>

<push_value> ::= "Y" <arguments>

<push_label> ::= "[" <arguments>

<arguments> ::= <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte> <byte>

<string> ::= <byte> | <byte> <string>

<byte > =  "\e0" .. "ÿ"
\fP
.fi
.RE
.SS "Instructions"
.PP
Since the virtual machine is stack-based, every instruction representing
an operation will pop it’s operands, and push the result to the stack.

.TS
 center,box;

l l l .
mnemonic	description	bnf object
_
not	Not, invert the bits of the operand	not
lnot	Logical not, invert the boolean interpretation of the operand	lnot
neg	Negate, switch the sign of the operand	neg
and	And, performs a binary AND operation between two operands	and
or	Or, performs a binary OR operation between two operands	or
xor	Exclusive or, performs a binary XOR operation between two operands	xor
shl	Left bitshift, shifts the bits of the operand to the left, appending zeroes	shl
shr	Right bitshift, shifts the bits of the operand to the right, appending zeroes	shr
land	Logical and, boolean interpretation of the logical AND on two operands	land
lor	Logical or, boolean interpretation of the logical OR on two operands	lor
add	Addition, adds the value of two operands	add
sub	Substraction, subtract the value of two operands	sub
mult	Multiplication, multiplies the value of two operands	mult
div	Division, return the dividend of the euclidian division of operand 1 by operand 2	div
mod	Modulo, return the rest of the euclidian division of operand 1 by operand 2	mod
gt	Greater than, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation	gt
ge	Greater than or equals to, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation	ge
lt	Less than, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation	lt
le	Less than or equals to, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation	le
eq	Equals, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation	eq
neq	Different from, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation	diff
updz	Update zero flag, pops the value from the stack and set it as the new zero flag	updz
push	Push value, push a 64 bits value to the stack	push\d\s-2value\s+2\u
push	Push relative address, push a 64 bits relative address to the stack, corresponding generally to a label	push\d\s-2label\s+2\u
push	Push from stack pointer relative address, push to the stack the 8 bytes found at the stack pointer + the address given as operand	push\d\s-2stack\s+2\u
pop	Pop empty, pop 8 bytes from the stack	pop
pop	Pop to stack pointer relative address, pop 8 bytes from the stack, and write them to the stack pointer + the address given as operand	pop\d\s-2stack\s+2\u
call	Call, op 8 bytes from the stack, register the new stack pointer, push the PC and stack pointer to the callstack, and jump to the value of the 8 bytes popped from the stack	call
ret	Return, reset the stack to the stack pointer, pop the callstack and jump to the address from the callstack pop	ret
jmp	Jump, pop an address from the stack, and set the PC to it	jmp
zjmp	Zero jump, pop an address from the stack, and if the zero flag is set, set the PC to it	zjmp
aff	Show, pop 8 bytes from the stack, apply a modulo 256 to the result, and write a single ascii character corresponding to the result	aff
affs	Show string, the first operand is a 64 bits integer corresponding to the number of caracters, that we will call n. The next n bytes are the ascii values of the characters that will be wrote	affs
.TE
.TB ""
.SH "Virtual Machine reference"
.SS "Introduction"
.PP
When defining an assembly language for the Franc C binary, we focused on
the ease of development of the compiler. We just had one requirement
that allowed an easy development of the virtual machine: our assembly
shall be
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stack-oriented_programming \fBat\fP \fIstack-based\fP,
drawing inspiration from the
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_virtual_machine \fBat\fP \fIJava Virtual
Machine (JVM)\fP.
.SS "Stack-Based vs Register-Based"
.TS
 center,box;

l l l .
Aspect	Stack-Based (fcvm)	Register-Based (e.g., Lua VM)
_
\fBStorage\fP	Operand stack (LIFO)	Virtual registers
\fBInstructions\fP	Compact, implicit operands, prone to errors	Explicit operand addressing
\fBComplexity\fP	Simple to implement	More complex logic
\fBBytecode Size\fP	Bigger overall (smaller instructions but way more quantiry)	Smaller overall
.TE
.TB ""

.PP
We chose a stack-based approach for the following reasons:

.IP 1.  4
\fBSimpler bytecode generation\fP: The compiler does not have to deal
with registers, knowing it does not have the capacity to remember
data while processing multiple operations.
.IP 2.  4
\fBMonotyped language\fP: The fact that the Franc C is an integer only
language allows for fixed-size parameter encoding. One of the main
advantages of the register-based approach is to allow for type
encoding.
.SS "Stack"
.PP
The stack shoud be implemented as a contiguous array of bytes with the
following characteristics:

.IP \(em 4
\fBData type\fP: Array of bytes
.IP \(em 4
\fBElement size\fP: 8 bytes per element (64-bit signed integers)
.IP \(em 4
\fBAlignment\fP: All operations push/pop exactly 8 bytes
.IP \(em 4
\fBStack size\fP: Always a multiple of 8 bytes
.RS
.TP
.ft I
Stack Management
.ft
.PP
To perform function calls, the VM must maintains the stack pointer. It
points to the start of the current stack local context. it is modified
by the instructions call and ret.
.RE
.SS "Initialization"
.PP
By default, the stack should be initialized with 8 bytes representing
the return value of the program. The default value is
implementation-defined.
.SS "Basic Operations"
.TS
 center,box;

l l l .
Operation	Description	Stack Effect
_
\fBPUSH\fP	Push 8 bytes onto the stack	SP unchanged, Counter += 1
\fBPOP\fP	Remove 8 bytes from the stack	SP unchanged, Counter -= 1
.TE
.TB ""
.SS "CallStack"
.PP
The \fBcallstack\fP manages function calls and returns, enabling the VM
to: - Navigate between functions - Restore execution context after
function returns - Maintain function-local state - Detect program
termination
.SS "Memory Layout"
.PP
The callstack can be implemented as a contiguous array of frame
records:
.br
By default, the callstack can be initialized as an \fBempty array\fP.

.PP
Example implementation : - \fBData type\fP: Array of tuples \fI[(PC, SP)]\fP -
\fBPC\fP (Program Counter): Return address (next instruction after CALL) -
\fBSP\fP (Stack Pointer): Stack state at call time

.TS
 center,box;

r r r l .
Frame	PC	SP	Description
_
0	0	0	Entry point
1	12	16	First function call
2	57	24	Current function locale start
.TE
.TB ""
.SS "Function call mechanism"
.PP
When executing \fIcall <address>\fP:

.IP 1.  4
\fBSave current context\fP: Push \fI(PC + 1, SP)\fP onto the call stack
.IP \(em 4
\fIPC + 1\fP: Address of the instruction following CALL
.IP \(em 4
\fISP\fP: Current stack pointer (preserves caller’s stack)
.IP 2.  4
\fBJump to function\fP: Set \fIPC = <address>\fP

.PP
When executing \fIRet\fP:

.IP 1.  4
\fBPop frame\fP: Retrieve \fI(saved_PC, saved_SP)\fP from call stack
.IP 2.  4
\fBRestore context\fP:
.IP \(em 4
Set \fIPC = saved_PC\fP (return to caller)
.IP \(em 4
Set \fISP = saved_SP\fP (restore stack state)
.IP 3.  4
\fBCheck termination\fP: If call stack is empty after pop, \fBterminate
program\fP
.SS "Zero flag"
.PP
The \fBZero Flag\fP is a single-bit register used for conditional branching
and control flow decisions.
.br
It stores the result of comparison and test operations,
.br
enabling the implementation of \fIif\fP and \fIwhile\fP.

.IP \(em 4
\fBData type\fP: \fIunsigned char\fP
.IP \(em 4
\fBDefault value\fP: \fI1\fP

.PP
The Zero Flag follows this logic:

.TS
 center,box;

l l .
Condition	Zero Flag Value
_
\fB!= 0\fP	\fI1\fP (true)
\fB== 0\fP	\fI0\fP (false)
.TE
.TB ""
.SS "End cases"
.PP
The program terminates normally when: - A \fIRet\fP instruction is executed
from the main function - The call stack becomes empty after the return

.PP
\fBVersion\fP : 1.2
.br
\fBLast update\fP : Novembre 2025
.br
\fBAuthor\fP : Acacademie Franc C’aise
.SH "Franc C Compiler"
.PP
The Franc C Compiler (FCC) is a toolchain that allows the compilation of
one or multiple Franc C Reference code (.fr) files into a single
bytecode executable by the FCVM program.
.SS "Parameters list and long descriptions"
.PP
\fIfcc FILESNAME [\-o|\-\-output FILENAME] [\-n|\-\-no\-larousse] [\-d|\-\-debug]\fP
.SS "FILESNAME"
.PP
The name of the input files. They need to be valid .fr source files.
.SS "--output FILENAME"
.PP
The name of the output bytecode file. Only considered without the
--debug flag.

.PP
Without the presence of this flag, the default output file is \fIa.fcp\fP.
.SS "--no-larousse"
.PP
By default, the FCC program compiles with the Larousse sources included.
See the custom-id:larousse \fBat\fP \fIlarousse\fP section.

.PP
This flag allows compilation without any files from the Larousse
library.
.SS "--debug"
.PP
This flags allows a debug output. Instead of writing the bytecode to a
file, a debug view of the assembly is wrote to the standard output
(/dev/stdout on most GNU/linux machines).
.SS "Larousse"
.PP
The Larousse is the standard library who accompanies the FCC program. If
the Larousse is to be included, the \fIfcc\fP program will search a folder
named \fIlarousse\fP, and then list in all subdirectories of level-1 the
\fI.fr\fP source files. Those found source files will be added to the input
list, and compiled.
.SS "Functioning"
.PP
The FCC program works with different steps.
.SS "Getting inputs"
.PP
The inputs are retrived via the Larousse library and the user-provided
files.
.SS "Lexing and parsing"
.PP
Each file is lexed into a list of token, then parsed into an Abstract
Syntax Tree (AST).

.PP
See custom-id:lexer-parsing-reference \fBat\fP \fIthe parsing reference\fP for more
information.

.PP
This step may fail. Each file being converted one after the other, if
one fails, the remaining ones are not even considered and an error is
returned.
.SS "Combination"
.PP
Each AST representing each files given for compilation is then combined.

.PP
In this step, the presence of a single principal function is verified,
and if no or multiple are found, an error is returned.

.PP
The result is a CAST (Combined Abstract Syntax Tree)
.SS "Compilation"
.PP
The compilation step is where the CAST is converted to a Context
datastructure, linked with an Assembly datastructure. It is not yet
bytecode, just a structure representing the final output.

.PP
The context structure is important for the next step.

.PP
This step is really linear, there is no acknowledgement of the rest of
the program when compiling a subpart.
.SS "Verifications"
.PP
This step ensures some basic verifications.

.PP
It takes the context from the previous step, and analyses it to find any
flaws.

.PP
For example, we check here if a function is invoked whilst it’s
reference is non-existant.
.SS "Output"
.PP
This step is the final one.

.PP
If the \fI\-\-debug\fP flag is given, it will take the structured assembly and
write it down just as it is stored.

.PP
Elsewise, it is converted to bytecode in really two small steps. The
first one is to convert labels to relative positions. The second one is
to convert everything to it’s bytecode relation.

.PP
Finaly, the resulted bytecode is written to the designated output file.
.SS "Justifications"
.PP
Regarding the lexing and parsing part, we decided so separate those to
allow for easier syntaxic sugar implementation and code readability.

.PP
The combination part was added to allow for multiple input files to be
grouped together before the compilation process. It is easier to do it
like this than to try and combine multiple outputed bytecodes together,
like the \fIld\fP program does when compiling a C program.

.PP
The compilation is linear, and therefore, we had to separate the
verification process from this step, to allow for a really easy-to-read
code. Having a linear compilation reduces the capacity of complex
program logic, but since the Franc C is a really simple imperative
language, it poses no problems to do it this way. We simply store some
context in a datastructure for the verification step to look in a global
point of view, instead of the linearly reduced point of view of the
compilation.

.PP
Finaly, we send the assembly to out output step, that is in charge of
converting it to the final bytecode, or to write it as a debug output.
.SS "Lexer & Parsing reference"
.PP
We have chosen Haskell to implement the lexing and parsing of the
language, as functional languages like Haskell make it particularly easy
to express, abstract, and combine parsers. This allows the Franc C to be
very verbose, which was definitely needed.

.PP
As we wanted to speed things up and release a first version as quickly
as possible, we decided to use a pre-existing parsing library
\fIMegaparsec\fP. The library offers fast parsing performance, is an
improved successor of \fIParsec\fP, and provides high-quality error messages
that greatly simplify debugging and development.

.PP
The \fILexer\fP and \fIParser\fP both refers to two different parts:

.IP \(em 4
The \fILexer\fP, which uses \fIMegaparsec\fP reads the source code’s syntax,
checks that it follows the correct grammar, then converts the text
into a list of tokens that the parser can later use. For example, the
code \fISi x est égale à 0, exécute le texte\fP would be transformed into
the list as \fI[If, Symbol "x", Operation Equals, Number 0, Then]\fP
.IP \(em 4
The \fIParser\fP, on the other hand, takes this list of tokens to
transform it into an \fBAbstract Syntax Tree\fP (AST). It uses Haskell’s
recursive principles to build that tree so that the compiler can later
use it to generate the final executable library.

.PP
The \fIParser\fP is only executed if the \fILexer\fP did not fail to lex the
targetted source code.
.SS "Safety measures"
.PP
The safety of the lexing process is ensured by \fIMegaparsec\fP, which
provides robust error handling and syntax checking. During the lexing
part, Megaparsec ensures the input strictly follows the defined grammar.
If the syntax is incorrect, the lexer fails gracefully by producing a
clear and descriptive error message, preventing the program from
continuing with an invalid input. If the syntax is valid, the lexer
successfully returns a well-structured list of tokens that can safely be
passed to the parser.

.PP
Since the Lexer always produces a clean and valid list of tokens, the
Parser does not include any additional safety checks on its input.
However, it makes sure that no more than \fBone\fP main function was
defined.
.SS "Error list"
.IP \(em 4
Grammar Error : Triggered when Megaparsec fails to lex or parse the
source code due to \fBinvalid syntax\fP.
.IP \(em 4
Double Main Definition : Raised when more than one main function was
defined in the program.
.SS "Known caveats"
.IP \(em 4
Lexing Error Printing : We are aware that Megaparsec does not always
display clear or accurate error messages in some cases. This issue is
due to our current lexing implementation rather than Megaparsec
itself, and should be fixed soon or later.
.IP \(em 4
Parsing Input : The parser currently does not validate its input and
fully relies on the lexer to always produce correct and consistent
tokens. While this works for now, it could lead to potential issues in
the future.
.SS "Compilation reference"
.PP
We are here referring to compilation as the compilation and the
verification step. Thus, in this section, the compilation step will
refer to both of these steps.

.PP
Given the simple nature of the logic of the Franc C language, we chose
here to implement a simple compilation.

.PP
The compilation step do not use any external dependencies.

.PP
We simply store a context that is updated throughout the compilation
process. This context contains :

.IP \(em 4
A list of variables, with their name, size, and position relative to
the stack pointer.
.IP \(em 4
A label counter, used to suffix labels with a number to distinguish
them.
.IP \(em 4
A list of function context, representing each definitions.
.IP \(em 4
A list of function context, representing each invoke.

.PP
The function context contains :

.IP \(em 4
The name of the function.
.IP \(em 4
A boolean value, used to describe if this function is either returning
something or of the void type ; or describes if this functions is
either assigning a value when invoking or not.
.IP \(em 4
A parameter count, that indicates the number of parameters in this
function.

.PP
The fact that the entire language is only integer-based facilitates the
concept of variable storage and parameter types. No types appear in the
compilation process, except the void type for function definitions and
invoke.
.SS "Function call parameters, return value and variables"
.PP
When a function is called, it have parameters, a return value and some
variables.

.PP
Here is a map of how they are stored in the stack:

.RS
.nf
┌─────────────────┐
│  Caller frame   │
├─────────────────┤
│  Parameters     │  ← PUSHREL/POPREL access
├─────────────────┤
│  Return Value   │  ← PUSHREL/POPREL access
├─────────────────┤  ← SP
│  Local vars     │  ← PUSHREL/POPREL access
├─────────────────┤
│  Temporaries    │  ← Stack top
└─────────────────┘

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBLocal variables\fP: Accessed via SP-relative offsets - \fIPUSHREL 0\fP: First
local - \fIPUSHREL 8\fP: Second local (8-byte offset) - \fIPOPREL N\fP: Store to
local at offset N

.PP
\fBReturn value and paramers\fP: Accessed via SP-relative offsets -
\fIPUSHREL \-8\fP: Return value - \fIPUSHREL \-16\fP: First parameter -
\fIPUSHREL \-24\fP: Second parameter
.SS "Safety measures"
.PP
During the compilation step, we check for user errors :

.IP \(em 4
No main function is given.
.IP \(em 4
Multiple main functions are given.
.IP \(em 4
A function is defined with the name of an already defined function.
.IP \(em 4
A function defines a parameter, or a variable, with the name of an
already existing, local to the function, parameter or variable.
.IP \(em 4
A function that is not referenced is invoked.
.IP \(em 4
A function that takes n parameters is invoked with more or less than n
parameters.
.IP \(em 4
A return value is given to a function of type void.
.IP \(em 4
An empty return is given to a function of type non-void.
.IP \(em 4
An assignement of the return of a void-typed function is made.
.SS "Intended undefined behaviours"
.RS
.TP
.ft I
Integer over/underflows
.ft
.PP
We do not devine the result of an integer overflow, or an integer
underflow. Our integers are defined solely as signed integers on 64
bits.

.PP
The minimum integer is defined as the maximum integer negated. In the C
programming language, it is defined as being one less, but in our
implementation of the negate sign “-”, we chose for parsing and lexing
simplicity to use the negate solely as an operation. Take the example
“-5”, we will not register it as the -5 number, but as the number 5 on
whom we apply the negate operation. This explains the fact that our
minimum integer is defined as the negated maximum integer.

.PP
The maximum integer is defined as 2\u\s-263\s+2\d - 1, or
9’223’372’036’854’775’807.
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Known caveats
.ft
.RS
.TP
.ft I
Lack of static analysis
.ft
.PP
The FCC compilation step do not implement any static analysis.

.PP
These are the direct cause of some -- sadly -- not implemented
behavious:

.IP \(em 4
We cannot check for direct division by zero. For example: (10 divisé
par 0) will not result in an error at compile-time.
.IP \(em 4
We cannot produce pre-computations. For example: (10 plus 10) will not
result in the value 20 at compile-time, but in the computation of 10
plus 10 at run-time. It is highly inefficient.
.RE
.RE
.SS "Readable assembly"
.PP
When the \fI\-\-debug\fP flag is passed, the output of the program is a
readable assembly written to the standard output.
.SS "Syntax of the readable assembly"
.PP
There are two cases in the syntax :

.IP \(em 4
label, they are written in the left margin, like so: \fIlabel_name:\fP
.IP \(em 4
instructions, they are written tabulated with four spaces, like so:
\fImnemonic [args]\fP
.SS "List of instructions"
.TS
 center,box;

l l l .
Operation	Parameter	Representation
_
label	name	\fIname:\fP
binary not		\fInot\fP
binary and		\fIand\fP
binary or		\fIor\fP
logical not		\fIlnot\fP
logical and		\fIland\fP
logical or		\fIlor\fP
exclusive or		\fIxor\fP
left bitshift		\fIshl\fP
right bitshift		\fIshr\fP
