.TE
.TB ""
.SH "Franc C Virtual Machine"
.SS "Input Descriptions"
.SS "Bytecode File Format"
.PP
The fcvm executes Franc C bytecode files.

.PP
They have the following header structure:

.RS
.nf
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Magic Number (4 bytes)              │  0x45 0x0C 0x45 0x0C
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Bytecode Instructions               │  Variable length
└─────────────────────────────────────┘

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBMagic Number\fP: \fI0x45 0x0C 0x45 0x0C\fP (4 bytes), as defined in the
custom-id:bytecode_reference \fBat\fP \fIFranc C bytecode reference\fP. - Validates file
format at VM initialization - Invalid magic number triggers immediate
error
.SS "Command-Line Interface"
.RS
.nf
\fC./fcvm <bytecode_file>
\fP
.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBExit Codes\fP:

.TS
 center,box;

l l .
Code	Description
_
\fI0\fP	Success (or custom return value = 0)
\fI1\-255\fP	Custom exit code (return value mod 256)
\fI84\fP	Any error (runtime or load-time)
.TE
.TB ""
.SS "Architecture Implementation"
.SS "Memory Layout"
.PP
The VM maintains three core structures:

.IP \(em 4
The operand stack
.IP \(em 4
The callstack
.IP \(em 4
Three specific registers
.RS
.TP
.ft I
Operand Stack
.ft
.PP
Stores temporary computation values, variables and function return
values.

.RS
.nf
┌─────────────┬─────────────┬─────────────┐
│  Element 0  │  Element 1  │  Element 2  │
│  (8 bytes)  │  (8 bytes)  │  (8 bytes)  │
└─────────────┴─────────────┴─────────────┘
      ↑
    Stack Top

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBProperties\fP: - Type: \fI[Word8]\fP (list of bytes) - Element size: 8 bytes
(64-bit) - Initial state: One zero element \fI[0x00 × 8]\fP - Max size:
1,000,000 elements (8 MB) - Operations: PUSH (prepend), POP (drop first
8 bytes)
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Call Stack
.ft
.PP
Manages function call frames.

.RS
.nf
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│  Frame: (PC: 0, SP: 0)          │  ← Entry
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│  Frame: (PC: 12, SP: 16)        │
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│  Frame: (PC: 57, SP: 24)        │  ← Current
└─────────────────────────────────┘

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBProperties\fP: - Type: \fI[(PC, SP)]\fP (list of tuples) - Frame content:
Return address (PC) + Stack pointer (SP) - Initial state: Empty \fI[]\fP -
Max depth: 1,000,000 frames

.PP
\fBBehavior\fP: - \fICALL\fP: Push \fI(PC+1, SP)\fP, jump to function - \fIRET\fP: Pop
frame, restore context, trim stack - \fBTermination\fP: Empty call stack
after \fIRET\fP → program ends
.RE
.TP
.ft I
Registers
.ft
.PP
\fBProgram Counter (PC)\fP - Points to current instruction in bytecode -
Initial: 8 (after 4-byte magic number) - Updates: \fI+1\fP (normal), \fI+9\fP
(with data), or jump target

.PP
\fBStack Pointer (SP)\fP - Marks base of current function’s stack frame -
Used for local variable access via \fIPUSHREL=/=POPREL\fP - Modified during
\fICALL\fP and \fIRET\fP

.PP
\fBZero Flag (ZF)\fP - Type: \fIWord8\fP (0 or 1) - Initial: 1 (true) -
Semantics: \fI0\fP if stack top = 0, else \fI1\fP - Set by \fIZFLAG\fP, tested by
\fIZJMP\fP
.RE
.SS "Instruction Set Overview"
.PP
The VM implements 40 opcodes across 7 categories:

.RS
custom-id:bytecode_reference \fBat\fP \fIBytecode Definition\fP.

.RE
.SS "Function Call Convention"
.PP
\fBCalling sequence\fP:

.RS
.nf
1. Push function address
2. Execute CALL
   → Saves (PC+1, SP) to call stack
   → Jumps to function

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBReturn sequence\fP:

.RS
.nf
1. Execute RET
   → Restores PC and SP
   → Trims stack to SP position
   → If call stack empty: program terminates

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBStack frame layout\fP:

.RS
.nf
┌──────────────────┐
│  Caller frame    │
├──────────────────┤  ← SP
│  Local function  │  ← Stack top
└──────────────────┘

.fi
.RE
.SS "Exceptions List"
.PP
All errors exit with code \fB84\fP and write to \fBstderr\fP.
.SS "Load-Time Errors"
.PP
\fBInvalid Magic Number\fP

.RS
.nf
Message: *** UNRECONIZED FILE FORMAT
Trigger: First 4 bytes ≠ 0x45 0x0C 0x45 0x0C

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBFile I/O Error\fP

.RS
.nf
Message: Error with file
Trigger: File not found, permission denied, or read error

.fi
.RE
.SS "Runtime Errors"
.PP
\fBArithmetic Errors\fP

.RS
.nf
Message: *** 0 CAN'T BE USE in this operation
Trigger: DIV or MOD with divisor = 0

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBStack & CallStack Overflow\fP

.RS
.nf
Message: *** STACK OVERFLOW
Trigger: Operand stack ≥ 1M elements OR call stack ≥ 1M frames
Cause:   Excessive PUSH without POP, or infinite recursion

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBStack Underflow\fP

.RS
.nf
Message: *** STACK ERROR
Trigger: Operation requires elements when stack_size ≤ 1
Affects: All binary/unary ops, CALL, JMP, ZJMP, AFF

.fi
.RE
.SS "Known Caveats"
.SS "Undefined Behaviors"
.PP
\fBInteger Overflow\fP - 64-bit signed integers wrap silently - Example:
\fIINT64_MAX + 1\fP → UNDEFINED - No error raised

.PP
\fBInvalid Opcodes\fP - Treated as NOP (PC += 1) - Corrupted bytecode may
execute partially - No detection mechanism
.SS "Performance Limitations"
.PP
\fBList-Based Stack\fP - Push/Pop: O(1) ✓ - SP-relative access: O(n) ✗ -
Impact: Slow for deep stacks (>1000 elements)

.PP
\fBString Operations\fP - \fIAFFS\fP creates intermediate structures - Linear in
string length (acceptable)
.SS "Portability Issues"
.PP
\fBPlatform Word Size\fP - \fIPC\fP and \fISP\fP use Haskell \fIInt\fP type - 32-bit
systems: Max ~2GB bytecode - 64-bit systems: Max ~8GB bytecode - Not a
practical concern for typical use

.PP
